                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Skyworld approaches virtual reality strategy from an appealing perspective, presenting its kingdoms as miniature fantasy worlds that seem to exist directly in front of the player. Instead of adapting a conventional strategy interface to VR, it embraces the feeling of interacting with a tabletop game. You can inspect landscapes, manage territories, prepare armies, and watch battles unfold from a commanding viewpoint. Developed by Vertigo Games and Wolfdog Interactive and published by Vertigo Games, it combines turn-based kingdom management with real-time battles to create a strategy experience specifically designed around virtual reality.\n\nThe campaign revolves around restoring magical worlds threatened by demonic invaders. These floating kingdoms depend on powerful magical cores, and the player gradually fights to reclaim territory while developing the resources necessary to support increasingly capable armies. The story is relatively straightforward and mostly exists to connect the different strategic scenarios, but the colorful fantasy setting gives the campaign plenty of personality. Dragons, unusual creatures, magical powers, and compact kingdoms make the environments resemble elaborate animated board-game pieces rather than conventional strategy maps.\n\nKingdom management forms the slower and more methodical side of the experience. Players oversee population, resources, structures, research, and military development while deciding how to expand their influence. New technologies and upgrades provide access to stronger options as the campaign progresses, encouraging experimentation with different army compositions. The economic systems are intentionally approachable rather than overwhelmingly complicated. This makes Skyworld suitable for players who enjoy strategic planning without necessarily wanting the enormous learning curve associated with more demanding grand strategy games.\n\nBattles introduce a significant change of pace because combat unfolds in real time. The forces assembled during the strategic portion become the tools used to defeat opposing armies, with units and spells requiring active deployment. Choosing the right forces is important, but successful combat also depends on reacting to enemy movements and determining when particular abilities should be used. Powerful creatures and magical attacks add spectacle without completely replacing tactical considerations. The transition between thoughtful turn-based development and faster combat gives the campaign a pleasant rhythm and prevents either component from dominating the entire experience.\n\nThe VR presentation remains Skyworld's defining feature. Looking down at a tiny kingdom while physically interacting with its elements creates an enjoyable sense of presence. Leaning closer to examine the environment or observing armies moving around the miniature landscape makes even routine strategic actions more engaging. The stylized graphics also complement this concept particularly well. Rather than pursuing realism, the game uses bright colors and exaggerated fantasy designs that reinforce the impression of controlling a living tabletop world.\n\nIts greatest weakness today comes from functionality that is no longer available. Online multiplayer and cooperative features were discontinued in 2025, meaning the game should now primarily be considered a single-player VR strategy experience. This is a meaningful reduction from the original package because competitive battles once gave players another reason to experiment with army combinations after becoming comfortable with the campaign. The remaining single-player material is still playable, but anyone interested mainly in multiplayer strategy will find the current experience considerably more limited.\n\nSteam Deck compatibility is effectively unsupported for practical purposes. Skyworld requires a PC VR headset and SteamVR environment, while Steam Deck is not designed to provide the game's required native VR setup. It should therefore not be purchased with the expectation of playing normally on the Deck's built-in screen or controls. Even though its underlying system requirements are relatively modest by current standards, the mandatory VR requirement makes a properly equipped PC the intended platform.\n\nSkyworld remains an imaginative example of how virtual reality can change the physical relationship between a player and a strategy game. Its economic management is accessible, its real-time battles add welcome energy, and the miniature fantasy presentation gives it an identity that ordinary mouse-driven strategy games cannot easily reproduce. The discontinued multiplayer functionality and dependence on older PC VR infrastructure reduce its appeal today, but the single-player campaign still offers something distinctive for VR owners who enjoy strategy. Rather than competing through enormous complexity, it succeeds through tactile interaction, charming presentation, and the satisfying sensation of ruling a tiny magical kingdom with your own hands. \n\nRating: 7/10",
